<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/01EW">Example <span data-tag="01EW">4.4.1.11</span>.</a> Let $F: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{D}}$ be a right fibration between $\infty $-categories. Then $F$ is an inner fibration (Remark <a data-tag="01GU" href="/tag/01GU">4.2.1.4</a>), and any isomorphism $u: D \rightarrow F(C)$ can be lifted to a morphism $\overline{u}: \overline{D} \rightarrow C$ in $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$, which is automatically an isomorphism by virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="019K" href="/tag/019K">4.4.2.11</a>. It follows that $F$ is an isofibration. Similarly, any left fibration of $\infty $-categories is an isofibration. For a more general statement, see Corollary <a data-tag="029G" href="/tag/029G">5.6.7.5</a>. </p>
